#
#   handleConnectionData was the deepest function in any of the six repositories at
#   NESTING DEPTH 7 -- a `while` around a `switch` around a `while` around a `switch`
#   -- and it is the milter read path, where X-6's connection deadlock lived. Now
#   depth 4 and 49 -> 30 lines, with the inner pass extracted as `drainBuffered`.
#
#   The check `if (self.connections.get(fd) == null) return;` APPEARED FIVE TIMES with
#   no name on it. It is not defensive noise: a handler can close the connection --
#   sendResponse removes it on a write failure -- so `conn` is freed memory the instant
#   dispatchPacket returns, and each of those five was load-bearing. It is now
#   `alive(fd)`, named once and explained once.
#
#   THE TEST CAME FIRST, which is the part worth insisting on. The property this
#   function turns on was documented only in a comment and covered by nothing: `feed`
#   reads at most 8192 bytes, so a larger packet returns .incomplete repeatedly, and
#   returning to the event loop on one waits for a kqueue edge that never arrives once
#   the peer is blocked writing. The new test sends one 24593-byte SMFIC_BODY packet
#   over a socketpair and calls the function ONCE. Teeth-checked against BOTH the old
#   and the new shape by changing `.incomplete => continue` to `return`: "expected
#   24593, found 0" in each case.
#
#   +27 net. Roughly 8 of those are the test's `recordBodyLen` helper and its module
#   variable, which sit outside the `test` block and so count as production -- the
#   limitation recorded in 11.31. The rest is two signatures and their doc comments,
#   bought with three levels of nesting and four unexplained repetitions.
